  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nSnow showers through Monday...Shortwave trough entering northwest\nWI tracks across far northern WI into Upper Michigan early Monday\nmorning. Already a few quick hitting snow showers developing\nacross northern WI ahead of the wave, with brief vsby reductions \nto less than 1 mile. Cold front tied to the trough will drop over \nnorthern WI, veers winds to N-NNW briefly before winds back back \nmore NW. Expect a burst of snow to impact Vilas County as this \nwind shift moves through given the increasing over-water \ninstability that will be present across western Lake Superior \n(lake EQLs 10-15kft) as 850mb temps drop toward -10c. Localized \nsnowfall up to 2 inches will occur before daybreak especially in \nfar northern Vilas County. Persistent NW flow ahead of next \nshortwave trough leads to a continuation of lake effect for the \nsnowbelts of Vilas County on Monday morning (1-2\") and will spread\nsnow showers and flurries farther south reaching into central and\nnortheast WI during the morning. \n\nAs the shortwave trough digs across into mid afternoon Monday, \nexpect the chance for some heavier snow showers or possibly a few \nsnow squalls to occur especially over northeast WI, including \nDoor County and perhaps as far south as Green Bay/Kewaunee early \nto mid afternoon as low-level lapse rates steepen from 8-9C/km \nwith SBCAPEs over 100 J/kg. Snow squall parameter is more than \nsufficient at 1-3. Though blended temperatures suggest flash \nfreeze is unlikely, some high res guidance shows pretty sharp \ncooling in wake of the period of heaviest snow showers. Going to \nhave to continue monitoring this snow squall potential for Monday \nafternoon.\n\nDrier air filters into the region Monday afternoon/evening as high\npressure arrives, allowing the lake effect snow activity to come \nto an end by late Monday evening.  \n\nRest of the week...Dry and cool Tuesday and Wednesday. Pattern\nbecomes very amplified Wednesday night through Thanksgiving night.  \nThere is uncertainty in how close main upper low tracks. 18z GFS\njust one idea, but did bring slug of mainly snow to eastern WI\nlate Wednesday night into Thursday morning. ECMWF and generally\nthe LREF keep the low farther southwest and keep main snow chances\nto south of our area, or at the least displaced more over southern\nWI. POPs continue to increase for Thanksgiving, but it is going to\ntake a couple days still to sort out details. If traveling from\nWisconsin to the south Wednesday night into Thanksgiving, stay \ntuned as this system could impact travel. Decent agreement system\nincreases as it shifts east into Friday. Main issue from that \ncould be developing low pressure system tracking along the east \ncoast. Dry conditions will eventually return to the western Great\nLakes, though eastern WI could still be clipped by some lingering\nprecip.  \n\nTemperatures: Late November temps have finally arrived as strong \nCAA occurs over the region into Monday. Temps will fall to a \nlittle below normal Monday and Tuesday, with highs mainly in the \n30s. Then temps slowly moderate mid-late week, with slightly above\nnormal readings by late this week.",
